# Proves exec_capture (step 3 of 3 toward self-hosting via --x64):
# spawns a real child process and captures its output. Uses `cmd /c
# echo ...` since it's guaranteed present on any Windows machine this
# backend targets, unlike nasm/gcc (whose own paths vary by machine).
#
# Hand-computed expected output:
#   running `cmd /c echo hello from exec_capture` and capturing its
#   stdout should give back "hello from exec_capture" (plus cmd's own
#   trailing newline, which rt_print_str will pass through as-is
#   before adding its own trailing newline -- so there may be a blank
#   line after, that's expected and not a bug).
#
# Expected output (first line):
#   hello from exec_capture

let out = exec_capture("cmd", "/c", "echo", "hello", "from", "exec_capture")
rt_print_str(out)
